        "thinking": "Port 3101 is the Node.js server itself (it's the main server, not a separate \"sidecar\" service). It runs the full Paperclip server including plugin install endpoints.\n\nSo the setup for local CF dev is:\n1. `pnpm dev` (or `bun run dev`) — starts the Node.js server on port 3101 (sidecar)\n2. `bun run dev:cf` — starts wrangler dev on port 8787 (CF Worker)\n\nFor the CF Worker to proxy plugin examples/install to the sidecar at 3101, both need to be running simultaneously.\n\n\nThe npm install error showing `@paperclipai/plugin-file-browser-example` suggests either the code hasn't been rebuilt with the local path logic, or the sidecar isn't running so the examples endpoint returns empty and the user is manually typing the package name into the install field.\n\nIf they're manually entering it, the CF handler receives `isLocalPath: false`, correctly identifies it as an npm package, and proxies the install request to the sidecar—which then attempts the installation.\n\nThe real issue is that this package isn't published to npm, so the install fails with a 404. The examples are meant to be installed via the \"Install Example\" button using local paths instead. Without the sidecar running on port 3101, the examples list endpoint returns an empty array since the proxy request fails.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the flow when the sidecar connection fails — the Durable Object stub gets invoked, but since there's no actual container running, it hits the connection refused error and the handler catches it by returning an empty array. The fetch call throws an exception when the sidecar isn't running, so I need to wrap the proxySidecar calls in try/catch blocks. For the examples endpoint, returning an empty array on error is fine, but for the install endpoint I should return a 503 with a helpful message explaining that the Node.js server needs to be running. I'll also add documentation noting that `bun run dev` needs to run alongside `bun run dev:cf` for plugin functionality to work.",
